gpiolib: acpi: Keep an IRQ's polarity off the GPIO line

acpi_dev_gpio_irq_wake_get_by() configures the GPIO behind a GpioInt
with gpiod_configure_flags(), passing GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW when the resource
is ActiveLow. It never requests the line, and gpiod_configure_flags()
only ever sets that flag; gpiod_free_commit() is what clears it. So the
flag stays on the line, and the next consumer to request it inherits
it and reads the line inverted, until it puts the line back.

On the Dell XPS 14 DA14260 the camera's CVS bridge takes its wake IRQ
from a GpioInt(Edge, ActiveLow, Shared) on GPP_F_20, and all four
CS35L57 amplifiers read their speaker ID from a GpioIo on the same pin.
The first amp to read it gets 1 while the line is low, then frees the
line, which clears the flag, and the other three read 0. So at every
boot one amp loads the tuning meant for a different set of speakers.

The interrupt's polarity is already applied as its trigger type, a few
lines further on, so the flag does nothing for the IRQ. Leave it off.

diff -ruN a/drivers/gpio/gpiolib-acpi-core.c b/drivers/gpio/gpiolib-acpi-core.c
--- a/drivers/gpio/gpiolib-acpi-core.c
+++ b/drivers/gpio/gpiolib-acpi-core.c
@@ -1048,6 +1048,15 @@
 			acpi_gpio_update_gpiod_flags(&dflags, &info);
 			acpi_gpio_update_gpiod_lookup_flags(&lflags, &info);
 
+			/*
+			 * The trigger type set below carries the interrupt's
+			 * polarity. The line isn't requested here, so an
+			 * active-low flag would stay on it until it's next
+			 * freed, and the next consumer to request it would
+			 * read its value inverted.
+			 */
+			lflags &= ~GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW;
+
 			snprintf(label, sizeof(label), "%pfwP GpioInt(%d)", fwnode, index);
 			ret = gpiod_set_consumer_name(desc, con_id ?: label);
 			if (ret)
